From 086dbb4fc59c3be2fe8a519fe0f5d5ae56d3cd8d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:27:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray: say when the Java runtime cannot run BD-J menus
 (Java 24 and later)

libbluray runs the Java code of a disc only under its own security manager
(org.videolan.BDJSecurityManager), which keeps that code to the disc, and fails closed when it
cannot install it (Libbluray.java). From Java 24 on (JEP 486) System.setSecurityManager()
always throws, and the reflective way libbluray 1.5.0 tries for Java 18 and later is not
there either. Measured on the build host with the BD-J jar of this tree and a disc made for
the test: with OpenJDK 25 "NoSuchMethodException: java.lang.System.implSetSecurityManager",
"Failed initializing SecurityManager", "Failed to initialize BD-J", bd_start_bdj() 0; with
Java 8 the manager is in place and the disc's code is kept to the disc.

Such a runtime loads, and the jar is found, so libbluray's own check (bdj_handled) says yes
and Kodi said in kodi.log that the menus can run; then they did not start, and the user saw
"BD-J menu error" with nothing about why.

The Java of JAVA_HOME is read from its "release" file (JAVA_VERSION), which every JDK and JRE
since 8 has. Java 24 or later is a state of its own, RUNTIME_TOO_NEW, said in kodi.log and to
the user like the other reasons, with what can run the menus: "JRE for BD-J menus" (Java 8)
from the LibreELEC repository. And when libbluray reports a BD-J error (BD_ERROR_BDJ) on a
disc whose Java was found and is not too new, that is said too (START_FAILED), once per disc,
pointing at kodi.log, where libbluray's own lines say why. A disc without BD-J titles, or a
Java that runs the menus, changes nothing; the error is still handled as before
(BD_EVENT_MENU_ERROR).

Two strings (#39884, #39885), in Spanish through Estuary (#32287, #32288) as the other texts of
BlurayJava.cpp.
